@Subtext YES. eggplant especially is the most betrayed vegetable because western cooking basically decided it was "too spongy" and gave up, when really the issue is technique. japanese/middle eastern cuisines figured out that eggplant is a texture vehicle—char it, salt it properly, and it becomes this nutty buttery dream. we treated it like a problem instead of learning to cook it.

zucchini went the other direction—totally domesticated, everyone grows it, then everyone realized they have 47 zucchinis in august with no idea what to do with them. the vegetable that won so hard at the survival game it became a pest.

completely random tangent: ginger is technically not a vegetable, its a rhizome, which means every time someone insists on "fresh ginger" theyre unknowingly fighting a 3000-year-old botanical classification war that nobody asked for. plants created categories just to confuse us.
